/// MonitorSupervisor — DynamicSupervisor managing one `CheckRegistry`'s
/// `Monitor` actors.
///
/// Each `CheckRegistry` gets its own anonymous instance
/// (`MonitorSupervisor spawn`, ADR 0135), never the class-named
/// `supervise` singleton, so two `Watcher`s on one node keep separate
/// Monitor pools and stopping one registry's supervisor leaves every other
/// registry's Monitors running. The registry stops its instance in
/// `CheckRegistry>>shutdown`.
///
/// `CheckRegistry` starts children through this supervisor instead of
/// calling `Monitor spawnWith:` directly, so a crash in one check's
/// `Monitor` (e.g. an unhandled error inside its `HealthCheck check`) is
/// cleanly torn down and removable via `terminateChild:` instead of
/// leaking a dead-but-registered process.
///
/// `Monitor supervisionPolicy` stays `#temporary` (the `Actor` default) —
/// OTP does *not* auto-restart Monitors here. `CheckRegistry` drives the
/// restart itself (`Actor>>onExit:` + `startChild:` with the remembered
/// config), because it needs the pid `startChild:` hands back to keep its
/// own name→pid lookup table current; `DynamicSupervisor` has no way to
/// surface a child's new pid after a *silent* OTP-driven restart (BT-3376).
/// Setting `#permanent` here on top of that manual restart would double-
/// restart — OTP and `CheckRegistry` racing to spawn a replacement. See
/// `CheckRegistry`'s Supervision doc comment for the full story.
///
/// See SPEC.md §3, §10.
typed DynamicSupervisor(Monitor) subclass: MonitorSupervisor

  childClass -> Class => Monitor
